/**
 * Renewal analytics — the PURE half (no DB, no React, no server-only imports),
 * unit-tested in test/renewal-analytics.test.ts. lib/renewal-analytics.ts loads
 * the real rows and hands them here as plain inputs.
 *
 * THE MODEL. Every paid service (VPS, game server, web hosting, business email,
 * domain, block-storage volume) is a chain of billing periods. Each time a
 * period ends the service owes a renewal; one such "renewal cycle" is
 * (service, due date) and gets exactly ONE outcome:
 *
 *   renewed    a renewal was collected for that period (wallet, card, PayPal or
 *              a mix) — sweep, admin "Renew now", or the customer's own renew.
 *              `recovered` marks one that was paid only after a failed charge.
 *   failed     INVOLUNTARY churn: auto-renew was on (the customer meant to keep
 *              it) but the money never arrived — the card declined / was
 *              unusable / the wallet was short, and the service was suspended,
 *              terminated, or removed unpaid.
 *   voluntary  VOLUNTARY churn: the customer chose to leave — auto-renew was off
 *              when the period ended (incl. "cancel at period end"), or they
 *              deleted / cancelled the service themselves (before term end or
 *              after it lapsed without any failed charge).
 *   admin      removed by Nxeon (fraud teardown, abuse/admin-suspension reaping,
 *              admin cleanup). Not a customer decision: excluded from rates.
 *   pending    the period has ended, nothing is collected yet and no outcome is
 *              final (inside the dunning / grace window).
 *   upcoming   the period has not ended yet.
 *
 * WHEN A CYCLE COUNTS. A decided cycle is placed at its `decidedAt` — the
 * EARLIER of its due date and the moment it was settled. An on-time renewal is
 * at its due date, a renewal paid in advance at the day it was paid, a failure
 * at its due date, and a cancellation before term end at the day of the
 * cancellation. So every number in a period is "renewals decided in that
 * period", and nothing ever lands in the future.
 */

